answer:
## Requirements to state first

- Runs the function once, after calls stop for `delay` ms
- Preserves arguments and `this`
- Exposes `cancel()`
- Optional `immediate` / leading-edge mode

---

## Solution

```js
function debounce(fn, delay = 300, { immediate = false } = {}) {
  let timerId = null;

  function debounced(...args) {
    const callNow = immediate && timerId === null;

    clearTimeout(timerId);
    timerId = setTimeout(() => {
      timerId = null;
      if (!immediate) fn.apply(this, args);
    }, delay);

    if (callNow) fn.apply(this, args);
  }

  debounced.cancel = () => {
    clearTimeout(timerId);
    timerId = null;
  };

  debounced.flush = function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timerId);
    timerId = null;
    fn.apply(this, args);
  };

  return debounced;
}
```

---

## Why `function` and not an arrow

```js
// ❌ An arrow has no own `this` — obj.method() would lose its receiver
const debounced = (...args) => { fn.apply(this, args); };
```

Using a `function` declaration lets `this` bind at the call site, so `debounce(obj.method)` still works when invoked as `obj.debounced()`. Interviewers look for this.

---

## The React follow-up

> "Now use it in a component."

```jsx
// ❌ A new debounced function every render — the timer resets constantly
const debouncedSave = debounce(save, 500);

// ✅ Stable identity, plus cancel on unmount
const debouncedSave = useMemo(() => debounce(save, 500), [save]);
useEffect(() => () => debouncedSave.cancel(), [debouncedSave]);
```

---

## Follow-ups to expect

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Difference from throttle? | Debounce waits for a pause; throttle fires at a fixed rate |
| Leading vs trailing edge? | The `immediate` flag above |
| How would you test it? | Fake timers — `vi.useFakeTimers()` and `advanceTimersByTime` |
| Return value? | It cannot return synchronously; return a promise if needed |

## Requirements

- Runs at most once per `interval`
- Leading call fires immediately
- **Trailing call fires** so the last event is not lost
- Exposes `cancel()`

---

## Naive version and its flaw

```js
// Works, but drops the final call — bad for scroll handlers
function throttle(fn, interval) {
  let last = 0;
  return function (...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= interval) {
      last = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    }
  };
}
```

If the user stops scrolling 50ms into a 200ms window, that final position is never handled — the UI ends up out of date.

---

## With a trailing call

```js
function throttle(fn, interval = 200) {
  let last = 0;
  let timerId = null;
  let lastArgs = null;

  function throttled(...args) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const remaining = interval - (now - last);

    if (remaining <= 0) {
      clearTimeout(timerId);
      timerId = null;
      last = now;
      fn.apply(this, args);
    } else {
      lastArgs = args;                 // remember the most recent call
      if (timerId === null) {
        timerId = setTimeout(() => {
          last = Date.now();
          timerId = null;
          fn.apply(this, lastArgs);    // fire the trailing call
        }, remaining);
      }
    }
  }

  throttled.cancel = () => {
    clearTimeout(timerId);
    timerId = null;
    last = 0;
  };

  return throttled;
}
```

---

## When to use which

| Scenario | Use |
|----------|-----|
| Search-as-you-type | Debounce |
| Autosave a draft | Debounce |
| Scroll position tracking | Throttle |
| Mouse-move drawing | Throttle |
| Infinite scroll trigger | Throttle (or `IntersectionObserver`) |
| Window resize handler | Debounce for the final size, throttle for live updates |

---

## The better answer for scroll

> "For scroll specifically I would reach for `requestAnimationFrame` instead — it syncs to the paint cycle rather than an arbitrary interval, so you never compute a position the browser will not render."

```js
let ticking = false;
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  if (ticking) return;
  ticking = true;
  requestAnimationFrame(() => {
    update(window.scrollY);
    ticking = false;
  });
}, { passive: true });
```

Mentioning `{ passive: true }` is a good extra — it tells the browser you will not call `preventDefault`, so scrolling is not blocked.

## Say this before you code

> "Four things matter: debouncing so we do not hammer the API, cancelling stale requests so an old response cannot overwrite a new one, caching so backspacing is instant, and keyboard plus ARIA so it is actually usable. I will build them in that order."

That framing alone separates you from most candidates.

---

## The hook

```jsx
function useTypeahead(query, delay = 300) {
  const [results, setResults] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const cache = useRef(new Map());

  const debounced = useDebounced(query, delay);

  useEffect(() => {
    const q = debounced.trim();
    if (!q) { setResults([]); return; }

    if (cache.current.has(q)) {        // instant on backspace
      setResults(cache.current.get(q));
      return;
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    setLoading(true);

    fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
      .then((r) => r.json())
      .then((data) => {
        cache.current.set(q, data);
        setResults(data);
      })
      .catch((e) => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setResults([]); })
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));

    return () => controller.abort();   // ← stale response can never win
  }, [debounced]);

  return { results, loading };
}
```

---

## Keyboard and ARIA

```jsx
function Typeahead() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const [active, setActive] = useState(-1);
  const { results } = useTypeahead(query);
  const listId = useId();

  function onKeyDown(e) {
    if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      e.preventDefault();
      setActive((i) => (i + 1) % results.length);
    } else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      e.preventDefault();
      setActive((i) => (i - 1 + results.length) % results.length);
    } else if (e.key === 'Enter' && active >= 0) {
      select(results[active]);
    } else if (e.key === 'Escape') {
      setQuery(''); setActive(-1);
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        role="combobox"
        aria-expanded={results.length > 0}
        aria-controls={listId}
        aria-activedescendant={active >= 0 ? `${listId}-${active}` : undefined}
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => { setQuery(e.target.value); setActive(-1); }}
        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
      />
      <ul id={listId} role="listbox">
        {results.map((r, i) => (
          <li
            key={r.id}
            id={`${listId}-${i}`}
            role="option"
            aria-selected={i === active}
            onMouseDown={() => select(r)}   // mouseDown, not click — beats blur
          >
            {r.label}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}
```

`onMouseDown` rather than `onClick` is a real detail: `blur` fires first and can close the list before the click registers.

---

## Follow-ups

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| 10,000 results? | Virtualise the list |
| Highlight the match? | Split on the query and wrap in `<mark>` |
| Cache invalidation? | LRU with a size cap, or React Query with `staleTime` |
| Race condition? | The `AbortController` — say this unprompted |

## Why not a scroll listener

```js
// ❌ Fires hundreds of times, forces layout on every read, needs throttling
onScroll = () => {
  if (el.scrollTop + el.clientHeight >= el.scrollHeight - 200) loadMore();
};
```

`IntersectionObserver` runs off the main thread, does not force reflow, and fires only when visibility actually changes.

---

## The hook

```jsx
function useInfiniteScroll({ hasMore, loading, onLoadMore }) {
  const sentinelRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!hasMore || loading) return;      // do not observe while busy

    const el = sentinelRef.current;
    if (!el) return;

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => { if (entry.isIntersecting) onLoadMore(); },
      { rootMargin: '200px' }             // start loading before it is visible
    );

    observer.observe(el);
    return () => observer.disconnect();
  }, [hasMore, loading, onLoadMore]);

  return sentinelRef;
}
```

The `loading` guard is what prevents firing the same page twice — the most common bug in this exercise.

---

## The component

```jsx
function Feed() {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [page, setPage] = useState(1);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(true);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  const loadMore = useCallback(async () => {
    setLoading(true);
    const res = await fetch(`/api/items?page=${page}`);
    const data = await res.json();

    setItems((prev) => [...prev, ...data.items]);
    setHasMore(data.hasMore);
    setPage((p) => p + 1);
    setLoading(false);
  }, [page]);

  const sentinelRef = useInfiniteScroll({ hasMore, loading, onLoadMore: loadMore });

  return (
    <>
      <ul>{items.map((i) => <li key={i.id}>{i.title}</li>)}</ul>

      {hasMore && <div ref={sentinelRef} aria-hidden="true" style={{ height: 1 }} />}
      {loading && <Spinner />}
      {!hasMore && <p>No more items</p>}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Follow-ups

**"The list gets slow after 2,000 items."** Combine with virtualisation — infinite scroll controls *fetching*, virtualisation controls *rendering*. They solve different problems and you often need both.

**"Offset pagination has a bug."** Correct — if an item is inserted while the user scrolls, `?page=2` shifts and an item is duplicated or skipped. Cursor pagination (`?after=<id>`) is stable.

**"Accessibility?"** Infinite scroll traps keyboard users away from the footer. Provide a "Load more" button as well, or use it instead — many teams do.

**"Back button?"** Returning to the feed restarts at page 1. Persist the loaded pages and scroll position, or use a data library that caches the pages.

---

## The library answer

```jsx
const { data, fetchNextPage, hasNextPage } = useInfiniteQuery({
  queryKey: ['items'],
  queryFn: ({ pageParam }) => fetchItems(pageParam),
  getNextPageParam: (last) => last.nextCursor,
});
```

In production, React Query's `useInfiniteQuery` handles caching, dedup and back-navigation. Say you would use it, then show you can build it.

## The three things interviewers check

1. Is the state one object or three booleans that can contradict each other?
2. Is there cancellation?
3. Do you know `fetch` resolves on a 500?

---

## Solution

```jsx
function useFetch(url, options) {
  const [state, setState] = useState({
    status: 'idle', data: null, error: null,
  });

  // Stringify so an inline options object does not re-trigger every render
  const optionsKey = JSON.stringify(options ?? {});

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!url) return;

    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState({ status: 'loading', data: null, error: null });

    (async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, { ...options, signal: controller.signal });

        // fetch only rejects on network failure — HTTP errors resolve normally
        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} ${res.statusText}`);

        const data = await res.json();
        setState({ status: 'success', data, error: null });
      } catch (error) {
        if (error.name === 'AbortError') return;   // expected on cleanup
        setState({ status: 'error', data: null, error });
      }
    })();

    return () => controller.abort();
    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
  }, [url, optionsKey]);

  return {
    ...state,
    isLoading: state.status === 'loading',
    isError: state.status === 'error',
  };
}
```

---

## Why one state object

```jsx
// ❌ Three booleans allow impossible combinations
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);
// loading: true AND data: set AND error: set — all at once. Now what renders?
```

A single `status` union makes invalid states unrepresentable. Mention that explicitly.

---

## Adding refetch

```jsx
const [reloadCount, setReloadCount] = useState(0);
const refetch = useCallback(() => setReloadCount((c) => c + 1), []);
// add reloadCount to the effect's dependencies
```

---

## Follow-ups

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Two components, same URL? | This fires two requests. React Query dedupes — that is the argument for it. |
| Retry? | Wrap in a loop with exponential backoff, and do not retry 4xx |
| Caching? | A module-level `Map` keyed by URL, with an eviction policy |
| Why not `useEffect(async () => …)`? | It returns a promise; React expects a cleanup function |
| Why abort rather than a boolean flag? | Abort also stops the network work, not just the state update |

---

## Close with the honest framing

> "I would not ship this. Server state needs caching, deduplication and revalidation, and rebuilding that badly is worse than adding React Query. But this is the right shape when you need something dependency-free."

## The idea

A container with fixed height and `overflow: auto`, an inner spacer of `totalHeight` so the scrollbar is correct, and only the visible rows rendered inside it.

---

## Fixed row height

```jsx
function VirtualList({ items, rowHeight = 40, height = 400, overscan = 3 }) {
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);

  const totalHeight = items.length * rowHeight;

  const startIndex = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - overscan);
  const visibleCount = Math.ceil(height / rowHeight) + overscan * 2;
  const endIndex = Math.min(items.length, startIndex + visibleCount);

  const visible = items.slice(startIndex, endIndex);

  return (
    <div
      style={{ height, overflowY: 'auto' }}
      onScroll={(e) => setScrollTop(e.currentTarget.scrollTop)}
    >
      {/* Spacer gives the scrollbar the full range */}
      <div style={{ height: totalHeight, position: 'relative' }}>
        {visible.map((item, i) => {
          const index = startIndex + i;
          return (
            <div
              key={item.id}
              style={{
                position: 'absolute',
                top: index * rowHeight,
                height: rowHeight,
                left: 0,
                right: 0,
              }}
            >
              {item.name}
            </div>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}
```

100,000 items, about 15 DOM nodes.

---

## The maths, stated plainly

```text
startIndex   = floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - overscan
visibleCount = ceil(containerHeight / rowHeight) + overscan * 2
offsetTop    = index * rowHeight
totalHeight  = itemCount * rowHeight
```

Overscan renders a few rows outside the viewport so fast scrolling does not show blank gaps.

---

## Variable heights — the follow-up

With unknown heights you cannot compute `startIndex` directly. The approach:

1. Estimate a height for every row
2. Measure rows as they render, with a `ResizeObserver` or `getBoundingClientRect`
3. Cache measured heights and keep a running prefix-sum of offsets
4. Binary search the prefix sums to find `startIndex` for a given `scrollTop`
5. Correct the total height as measurements arrive

Say you would use TanStack Virtual for this — hand-rolling variable heights correctly is genuinely hard, and knowing that is the senior answer.

---

## Follow-ups

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Why not `useState` for scrollTop? | It re-renders per scroll event. A ref plus `rAF` batching is smoother. |
| Horizontal? | Same maths on the X axis |
| Ctrl+F? | Broken — provide your own search |
| Accessibility? | `role="grid"` with `aria-rowcount` and `aria-rowindex` |
| Sticky headers? | Render them outside the scrolling spacer |

---

## When not to

Below ~100 rows it is pure overhead. Between 100 and 1,000, measure. Pagination is often the simpler product answer.

## The focus trap hook

```jsx
const FOCUSABLE = 'a[href], button:not([disabled]), textarea, input:not([disabled]), select, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])';

function useFocusTrap(ref, isOpen) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen) return;

    const previouslyFocused = document.activeElement;
    const node = ref.current;

    // Move focus in
    const focusables = () => [...node.querySelectorAll(FOCUSABLE)];
    (focusables()[0] ?? node).focus();

    function onKeyDown(e) {
      if (e.key !== 'Tab') return;

      const items = focusables();          // re-query: content can change
      if (items.length === 0) { e.preventDefault(); return; }

      const first = items[0];
      const last = items[items.length - 1];

      if (e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === first) {
        e.preventDefault();
        last.focus();                       // wrap backwards
      } else if (!e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === last) {
        e.preventDefault();
        first.focus();                      // wrap forwards
      }
    }

    node.addEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);
    return () => {
      node.removeEventListener('keydown', onKeyDown);
      previouslyFocused?.focus();           // restore
    };
  }, [ref, isOpen]);
}
```

Re-querying inside the handler matters — a modal whose content changes while open would otherwise trap against a stale list.

---

## The modal

```jsx
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }) {
  const ref = useRef(null);
  const titleId = useId();
  useFocusTrap(ref, isOpen);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen) return;
    const onKey = (e) => e.key === 'Escape' && onClose();
    document.addEventListener('keydown', onKey);

    const prev = document.body.style.overflow;
    document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';

    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', onKey);
      document.body.style.overflow = prev;
    };
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);

  if (!isOpen) return null;

  return createPortal(
    <div className="overlay" onClick={onClose}>
      <div
        ref={ref}
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        aria-labelledby={titleId}
        tabIndex={-1}
        onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}
      >
        <h2 id={titleId}>{title}</h2>
        {children}
      </div>
    </div>,
    document.body
  );
}
```

---

## The checklist to recite

| Requirement | Why |
|-------------|-----|
| Portal | Escapes `overflow: hidden` and z-index stacking |
| `role="dialog"` + `aria-modal` | Announced correctly, background ignored |
| `aria-labelledby` | The dialog has a name |
| Focus in on open | Keyboard users start inside |
| **Tab trapped** | Cannot reach the page behind |
| Focus restored on close | Returns to the trigger |
| Escape closes | Expected behaviour |
| Body scroll locked | Background must not scroll |

---

## The senior close

> "In production I would use Radix or React Aria. `inert` on the background, scroll-lock on iOS and nested dialogs all have edge cases that are not worth reimplementing. The native `<dialog>` element with `showModal()` also gives you the top layer, trapping and Escape for free."

## The two-state insight

```jsx
const [rating, setRating] = useState(0);
const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(0);

const display = hovered || rating;   // preview wins while hovering
```

One state variable cannot express "showing 4 on hover but 3 is actually selected". Interviewers watch for this.

---

## Accessible implementation

```jsx
function StarRating({ max = 5, value, onChange, label = 'Rating' }) {
  const [hovered, setHovered] = useState(0);
  const name = useId();
  const display = hovered || value;

  return (
    <fieldset onMouseLeave={() => setHovered(0)} className="stars">
      <legend>{label}</legend>

      {Array.from({ length: max }, (_, i) => {
        const starValue = i + 1;
        return (
          <label key={starValue} onMouseEnter={() => setHovered(starValue)}>
            {/* Visually hidden, but real — arrow keys and screen readers work */}
            <input
              type="radio"
              name={name}
              value={starValue}
              checked={value === starValue}
              onChange={() => onChange(starValue)}
              className="sr-only"
            />
            <span aria-hidden="true" className={starValue <= display ? 'filled' : 'empty'}>
              ★
            </span>
            <span className="sr-only">{starValue} of {max} stars</span>
          </label>
        );
      })}
    </fieldset>
  );
}
```

Using real radio inputs means arrow-key navigation, form submission and screen reader announcement all work without a line of custom code. A `div` with `onClick` gives you none of that.

---

## Follow-ups

**"Half stars?"** Split each star into two half-width hit areas, or compute from the pointer's X offset:

```jsx
function onMouseMove(e, starValue) {
  const { left, width } = e.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
  const isLeftHalf = e.clientX - left < width / 2;
  setHovered(isLeftHalf ? starValue - 0.5 : starValue);
}
```

For display, overlay a clipped filled star on an empty one using `width: 50%` and `overflow: hidden`.

**"Read-only mode?"** Render `<output>` or a `<span>` with `aria-label="Rated 4 out of 5"` — not disabled inputs, which are skipped by some screen readers.

**"Clear the rating?"** Clicking the currently selected star sets it back to 0. Add a visible "Clear" control too, since that gesture is not discoverable.

**"Why not `onClick` on a div?"** Because you would then owe: `tabIndex`, `role="radiogroup"`, `role="radio"`, `aria-checked`, arrow-key handling, Space and Enter handling, and roving tabindex. The native element does all of it.

---

## What this question is really testing

Hover-vs-selected state separation, and whether you reach for semantic HTML before ARIA. It looks trivial and most candidates ship something inaccessible.

## Core mechanism

```jsx
function Carousel({ slides, autoPlayMs = 0 }) {
  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);
  const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);

  const next = useCallback(() => setIndex((i) => (i + 1) % slides.length), [slides.length]);
  const prev = () => setIndex((i) => (i - 1 + slides.length) % slides.length);

  // Autoplay — pause on hover, focus, and when the tab is hidden
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!autoPlayMs || paused) return;
    const id = setInterval(next, autoPlayMs);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [autoPlayMs, paused, next]);

  return (
    <section
      aria-roledescription="carousel"
      aria-label="Featured"
      onMouseEnter={() => setPaused(true)}
      onMouseLeave={() => setPaused(false)}
      onFocusCapture={() => setPaused(true)}    // ← keyboard users
      onBlurCapture={() => setPaused(false)}
      onKeyDown={(e) => {
        if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') next();
        if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') prev();
      }}
    >
      <div className="viewport" style={{ overflow: 'hidden' }}>
        <div
          className="track"
          style={{
            display: 'flex',
            transform: `translateX(-${index * 100}%)`,
            transition: 'transform 300ms ease',
          }}
        >
          {slides.map((s, i) => (
            <div
              key={s.id}
              style={{ flex: '0 0 100%' }}
              role="group"
              aria-roledescription="slide"
              aria-label={`${i + 1} of ${slides.length}`}
              aria-hidden={i !== index}
            >
              <img src={s.src} alt={s.alt} />
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>

      <button onClick={prev} aria-label="Previous slide">‹</button>
      <button onClick={next} aria-label="Next slide">›</button>

      <div aria-live="polite" className="sr-only">
        Slide {index + 1} of {slides.length}
      </div>
    </section>
  );
}
```

---

## The details that earn marks

| Detail | Why |
|--------|-----|
| `onFocusCapture` pauses | Otherwise a keyboard user can never finish reading a slide |
| `aria-hidden` on inactive slides | Screen readers should not read all five at once |
| `aria-live="polite"` | Announces the change without stealing focus |
| Modulo wrapping | `(i - 1 + len) % len` — the `+ len` avoids a negative index |
| Reduced motion | Disable the transition and autoplay entirely |

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .track { transition: none; }
}
```

---

## Follow-ups

**"Infinite loop without the snap-back?"** Clone the first and last slides at each end, and jump without transition when you land on a clone. Or use `scroll-snap` and let the browser handle it.

**"Touch swipe?"** Track `pointerdown` / `pointermove` / `pointerup`, translate by the drag delta, and commit to the next slide if the drag exceeds a threshold — otherwise spring back.

**"Many heavy images?"** Render only the current slide plus its neighbours, and `loading="lazy"` the rest.

---

## The honest note

CSS `scroll-snap-type: x mandatory` gives you a swipeable, keyboard-accessible carousel with almost no JavaScript. Mentioning it shows you reach for the platform before a library.

## The simple version

```jsx
function ReorderableList({ items, onReorder }) {
  const dragIndex = useRef(null);

  function handleDragStart(index) {
    dragIndex.current = index;
  }

  function handleDragOver(e, overIndex) {
    e.preventDefault();                       // required to allow a drop
    const from = dragIndex.current;
    if (from === null || from === overIndex) return;

    const next = [...items];
    const [moved] = next.splice(from, 1);
    next.splice(overIndex, 0, moved);

    dragIndex.current = overIndex;            // track the new position
    onReorder(next);
  }

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map((item, i) => (
        <li
          key={item.id}                        // NEVER the index here
          draggable
          onDragStart={() => handleDragStart(i)}
          onDragOver={(e) => handleDragOver(e, i)}
          onDragEnd={() => { dragIndex.current = null; }}
        >
          {item.label}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

`e.preventDefault()` in `dragOver` is mandatory — without it the browser refuses the drop, and it is the number one reason a first attempt "does nothing".

---

## Why `key={item.id}` is critical here

The list reorders on every drag movement. Index keys would make React reuse the wrong DOM nodes, so the dragged element visually detaches from the data. This exercise is the clearest demonstration of why index keys are dangerous.

---

## Where hand-rolled breaks down

| Problem | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Touch devices** | HTML5 drag events do not fire. You need Pointer Events. |
| **Keyboard** | No way to reorder without a mouse |
| **Screen readers** | No announcement of what moved where |
| **Auto-scroll** | Dragging to the edge of a long list should scroll |
| **Drag preview** | The default ghost image is often wrong |
| **Nested lists** | Drop-target resolution gets genuinely hard |

