## The accessible pattern

```jsx
function Tabs({ tabs, defaultId }) {
  const [activeId, setActiveId] = useState(defaultId ?? tabs[0].id);
  const baseId = useId();
  const refs = useRef({});

  function onKeyDown(e) {
    const i = tabs.findIndex((t) => t.id === activeId);
    let nextIndex = null;

    if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') nextIndex = (i + 1) % tabs.length;
    if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') nextIndex = (i - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length;
    if (e.key === 'Home') nextIndex = 0;
    if (e.key === 'End') nextIndex = tabs.length - 1;

    if (nextIndex !== null) {
      e.preventDefault();
      const nextId = tabs[nextIndex].id;
      setActiveId(nextId);
      refs.current[nextId]?.focus();      // focus follows selection
    }
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <div role="tablist" aria-label="Sections" onKeyDown={onKeyDown}>
        {tabs.map((tab) => (
          <button
            key={tab.id}
            ref={(el) => { refs.current[tab.id] = el; }}
            role="tab"
            id={`${baseId}-tab-${tab.id}`}
            aria-selected={activeId === tab.id}
            aria-controls={`${baseId}-panel-${tab.id}`}
            tabIndex={activeId === tab.id ? 0 : -1}   // ← roving tabindex
            onClick={() => setActiveId(tab.id)}
          >
            {tab.label}
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>

      {tabs.map((tab) => (
        <div
          key={tab.id}
          role="tabpanel"
          id={`${baseId}-panel-${tab.id}`}
          aria-labelledby={`${baseId}-tab-${tab.id}`}
          hidden={activeId !== tab.id}
          tabIndex={0}
        >
          {tab.content}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

---

## Roving tabindex, explained

Without it, Tab steps through every tab button — with ten tabs, a keyboard user presses Tab ten times to reach the content.

With it, only the active tab has `tabIndex={0}`. Tab enters the tablist once, arrow keys move within it, Tab again moves to the panel. This is the standard for any grouped widget: tabs, radio groups, toolbars, menus.

---

## The ARIA contract

```text
tablist  ─ contains the tabs
  tab    ─ aria-selected, aria-controls → panel id
tabpanel ─ aria-labelledby → tab id, hidden when inactive
```

The ids must point at each other in both directions.

---

## Follow-ups

**"Should selection follow focus?"** For cheap panels, yes — arrow keys change the panel immediately. For panels that fetch data, decouple them: arrows move focus, Enter or Space selects. Knowing both patterns exist is the senior answer.

**"Lazy-render panels?"** `hidden` keeps them mounted, which preserves state but renders everything. Conditionally rendering only the active panel is cheaper but resets state. Choose per use case and say why.

**"Put the tab in the URL?"** Yes for anything shareable — `?tab=billing`. Same reasoning as any view state.

**"Overflowing tabs?"** Either scroll the tablist horizontally with scroll-snap, or collapse the overflow into a "More" menu.

