Whether you know that a "simple" tabs component has a published specification. Quoting the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices pattern signals you have built real components, not just styled divs.

## The full implementation

```jsx
function useLocalStorage(key, initialValue) {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(() => {
    try {
      const item = localStorage.getItem(key);
      return item ? JSON.parse(item) : initialValue;
    } catch {
      return initialValue;
    }
  });

  const setStoredValue = useCallback((next) => {
    setValue((prev) => {
      const resolved = next instanceof Function ? next(prev) : next;
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(resolved));
        // The native storage event does NOT fire in this tab — dispatch our own
        window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('local-storage', { detail: { key } }));
      } catch {
        // quota exceeded, private mode
      }
      return resolved;
    });
  }, [key]);

  useEffect(() => {
    function sync(e) {
      // storage event (other tabs) or our CustomEvent (this tab)
      if (e.key && e.key !== key) return;
      if (e.detail && e.detail.key !== key) return;

      try {
        const item = localStorage.getItem(key);
        setValue(item ? JSON.parse(item) : initialValue);
      } catch { /* ignore */ }
    }

    window.addEventListener('storage', sync);        // other tabs
    window.addEventListener('local-storage', sync);  // this tab
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('storage', sync);
      window.removeEventListener('local-storage', sync);
    };
  }, [key, initialValue]);

  return [value, setStoredValue];
}
```

---

## The four details interviewers probe

**1. Lazy initialiser.** `useState(() => …)` reads storage once, not on every render.

**2. `try/catch` on both sides.** Reading throws on corrupt JSON; writing throws on quota exceeded and in Safari private mode. An unguarded read crashes the app at startup.

**3. The storage event asymmetry.** It fires in *other* tabs only. Two components in the same tab using the same key would drift out of sync without the custom event.

**4. Functional updates.** Accepting `next instanceof Function` keeps the API identical to `useState`.

---

## The SSR problem

```jsx
// ❌ localStorage does not exist on the server → crash or hydration mismatch
useState(() => localStorage.getItem(key));

// ✅ Match the server on first render, correct after mount
const [value, setValue] = useState(initialValue);
useEffect(() => {
  const stored = localStorage.getItem(key);
  if (stored) setValue(JSON.parse(stored));
}, [key]);
```

The cost is one frame showing the default. For themes, the standard fix is a small blocking script in `<head>` that sets a class before React runs.

---

## Follow-ups

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| Large objects? | `localStorage` is synchronous and blocks the main thread — debounce writes, or use IndexedDB |
| `sessionStorage`? | Same code, different API — parameterise the storage object |
| Cross-tab without storage? | `BroadcastChannel` is the purpose-built API |
| Auth token? | Never here — any script on the page can read it. Use an `httpOnly` cookie. |
| Remove a key? | Add a `remove()` that calls `removeItem` and dispatches the event |

## Implementation

```js
async function retry(fn, {
  attempts = 3,
  baseDelay = 300,
  maxDelay = 10_000,
  shouldRetry = () => true,
  signal,
} = {}) {
  let lastError;

  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < attempts; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await fn({ attempt });
    } catch (error) {
      lastError = error;

      if (signal?.aborted) throw error;
      if (attempt === attempts - 1) break;         // no delay after the last try
      if (!shouldRetry(error, attempt)) throw error;

      // Exponential: 300, 600, 1200, 2400…  plus jitter
      const exponential = Math.min(baseDelay * 2 ** attempt, maxDelay);
      const jitter = Math.random() * exponential * 0.3;

      await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const id = setTimeout(resolve, exponential + jitter);
        signal?.addEventListener('abort', () => {
          clearTimeout(id);
          reject(signal.reason);
        }, { once: true });
      });
    }
  }

  throw lastError;
}
```

---

## Using it

```js
const data = await retry(
  async () => {
    const res = await fetch('/api/data');
    if (!res.ok) {
      const err = new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
      err.status = res.status;
      throw err;
    }
    return res.json();
  },
  {
    attempts: 4,
    // Retry server errors and rate limits; never retry a 400 or 404
    shouldRetry: (e) => !e.status || e.status >= 500 || e.status === 429,
  }
);
```

---

## Why jitter matters

Without it, a thousand clients that all failed at the same moment retry at exactly the same moment — a **thundering herd** that re-kills the service just as it recovers. Randomising 0–30% of the delay spreads them out.

This is the detail that separates a textbook answer from someone who has run this in production.

---

## Why not retry 4xx

| Status | Retry? | Why |
|--------|--------|-----|
| Network failure | ✅ | Transient |
| 500, 502, 503, 504 | ✅ | Server-side, likely transient |
| 429 Too Many Requests | ✅ | But honour `Retry-After` |
| 400, 404, 422 | ❌ | The request is wrong; retrying wastes capacity |
| 401 | ❌ | Refresh the token, then retry once |

---

## Idempotency — say this unprompted

> "Retrying a non-idempotent request is dangerous. A `POST /payments` that timed out may have succeeded, so retrying charges twice. For those I would send an idempotency key so the server can deduplicate."

GET, PUT and DELETE are naturally idempotent. POST is not.

---

## In React

React Query has this built in — `retry: 3` with exponential backoff by default, and `retry: (count, error) => …` for the 4xx rule. Implement it when asked; use the library when shipping.

## Why not the JSON trick

```js
const clone = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj));
```

It silently corrupts data:

| Input | Becomes |
|-------|---------|
| `new Date()` | An ISO string |
| `undefined` | The key disappears |
| `NaN`, `Infinity` | `null` |
| Functions | The key disappears |
| `Map`, `Set` | `{}` |
| `BigInt` | **Throws** |
| Circular reference | **Throws** |

It is fine for plain JSON-shaped data. Say that explicitly rather than dismissing it.

---

## The implementation

```js
function deepClone(value, seen = new WeakMap()) {
  // Primitives and functions are returned as-is
  if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') return value;

  // Circular reference — return the clone we already made
  if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value);

  // Built-ins that need special handling
  if (value instanceof Date) return new Date(value.getTime());
  if (value instanceof RegExp) return new RegExp(value.source, value.flags);

  if (value instanceof Map) {
    const result = new Map();
    seen.set(value, result);
    value.forEach((v, k) => result.set(deepClone(k, seen), deepClone(v, seen)));
    return result;
  }

  if (value instanceof Set) {
    const result = new Set();
    seen.set(value, result);
    value.forEach((v) => result.add(deepClone(v, seen)));
    return result;
  }

  if (Array.isArray(value)) {
    const result = [];
    seen.set(value, result);                     // set BEFORE recursing
    value.forEach((v, i) => { result[i] = deepClone(v, seen); });
    return result;
  }

  // Plain object — preserve the prototype
  const result = Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(value));
  seen.set(value, result);
  for (const key of Reflect.ownKeys(value)) {    // includes symbols
    result[key] = deepClone(value[key], seen);
  }
  return result;
}
```

---

## The two details that matter

**Register in the map before recursing.** Otherwise `obj.self = obj` recurses forever — you must record the clone before descending into its children.

**`WeakMap`, not `Map`.** Keys are weakly held, so the cloned graph can be garbage collected. A `Map` would pin every source object in memory.

---

## The built-in

```js
const clone = structuredClone(obj);
```

Handles cycles, Dates, Maps, Sets, RegExp, typed arrays, `ArrayBuffer` and Blobs. It **cannot** clone functions, DOM nodes, or class instances with their prototype — it throws on functions and flattens class instances to plain objects.

Available in all modern browsers and Node 17+. Mentioning it first, then implementing by hand, is the right order.

---

## The React angle

> "In React I would rarely deep clone. Immutable updates only copy the path that changed, which is far cheaper — deep cloning a large state tree on every update is a performance problem, not a solution. If nesting is deep enough to make spreads painful, I would use Immer or normalise the shape."

## Implementation

```js
function promiseAll(iterable) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const items = Array.from(iterable);
    const results = new Array(items.length);
    let settled = 0;

    if (items.length === 0) return resolve([]);   // ← the edge case people miss

    items.forEach((item, index) => {
      // Promise.resolve handles non-promise values too
      Promise.resolve(item).then(
        (value) => {
          results[index] = value;                 // by index, not push
          settled++;
          if (settled === items.length) resolve(results);
        },
        reject                                     // first rejection wins
      );
    });
  });
}
```

---

## The three things being tested

**1. Order preservation.** `results[index] = value` rather than `results.push(value)`. With push, a fast third promise lands at position 0.

**2. Counting, not checking.** You cannot inspect whether a promise is pending, so you count completions.

**3. The empty array.** `Promise.all([])` resolves immediately with `[]`. Without the guard, the counter never reaches 0 and the promise hangs forever.

A fourth, if you want to impress: `Promise.resolve(item)` means non-promise values pass through, matching the spec.

---

## The siblings

```js
// allSettled — never rejects
function promiseAllSettled(items) {
  return promiseAll(
    Array.from(items).map((p) =>
      Promise.resolve(p).then(
        (value) => ({ status: 'fulfilled', value }),
        (reason) => ({ status: 'rejected', reason })
      )
    )
  );
}

// race — first to settle either way
function promiseRace(items) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    for (const item of items) Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, reject);
  });
}

// any — first to SUCCEED; rejects only if all fail
function promiseAny(items) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const arr = Array.from(items);
    const errors = new Array(arr.length);
    let failed = 0;

    if (arr.length === 0) return reject(new AggregateError([], 'All promises were rejected'));

    arr.forEach((item, i) => {
      Promise.resolve(item).then(resolve, (err) => {
        errors[i] = err;
        if (++failed === arr.length) {
          reject(new AggregateError(errors, 'All promises were rejected'));
        }
      });
    });
  });
}
```

Once `resolve` or `reject` has been called, later calls are no-ops — which is why `race` can be that short.

---

## Common follow-up

> "Does `Promise.all` cancel the others when one rejects?"

**No.** They all keep running; their results are simply discarded. Promises are not cancellable. To actually stop the work you need an `AbortController` shared across the requests — a good detail to volunteer.

---

## The React relevance

```js
// Sequential — 900ms
const a = await fetchA(); const b = await fetchB(); const c = await fetchC();

// Parallel — 300ms
const [a, b, c] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB(), fetchC()]);
```

Request waterfalls in effects are one of the most common real performance problems in React apps.

## Basic version

```js
function memoize(fn, keyFn = (...args) => JSON.stringify(args)) {
  const cache = new Map();

  function memoized(...args) {
    const key = keyFn(...args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);

    const result = fn.apply(this, args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  memoized.cache = cache;
  memoized.clear = () => cache.clear();
  return memoized;
}
```

`cache.has(key)` rather than checking the value handles a legitimately cached `undefined`.

---

## Why the default key function is flawed

```js
JSON.stringify([{ a: 1, b: 2 }])   // '[{"a":1,"b":2}]'
JSON.stringify([{ b: 2, a: 1 }])   // '[{"b":2,"a":1}]'  ← different key, same data
```

It also drops functions and `undefined`, and throws on circular structures. For single-argument object keys, a `WeakMap` is better — it keys by identity and lets entries be garbage collected.

---

## With LRU eviction

```js
function memoize(fn, { maxSize = 100, keyFn = (...a) => JSON.stringify(a) } = {}) {
  const cache = new Map();   // Map preserves insertion order — that is the trick

  return function (...args) {
    const key = keyFn(...args);

    if (cache.has(key)) {
      const value = cache.get(key);
      cache.delete(key);
      cache.set(key, value);        // re-insert → now the most recent
      return value;
    }

    const result = fn.apply(this, args);
    cache.set(key, result);

    if (cache.size > maxSize) {
      cache.delete(cache.keys().next().value);   // oldest key is first
    }

    return result;
  };
}
```

Map's guaranteed insertion order gives you an LRU in a few lines — a neat thing to point out.

---

## Async memoisation, and its trap

```js
function memoizeAsync(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();

  return async function (...args) {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);

    // Cache the PROMISE, not the result — this deduplicates concurrent calls
    if (!cache.has(key)) {
      cache.set(key, fn.apply(this, args).catch((err) => {
        cache.delete(key);      // do not cache failures forever
        throw err;
      }));
    }
    return cache.get(key);
  };
}
```

Caching the promise means ten simultaneous calls produce one network request. Caching the resolved value means ten requests fire before the first resolves.

---

## When memoisation is wrong

| Do not memoise | Why |
|----------------|-----|
| Impure functions | Cached output goes stale |
| Cheap operations | The key building costs more |
| Rarely-repeated arguments | Pure memory growth, no hits |
| Anything time-sensitive | Needs a TTL, not plain memoisation |

In React, `useMemo` is this idea with a cache size of one, keyed by the dependency array.

## The wrong way

```jsx
// ❌ Drifts. setInterval is not precise, and background tabs throttle
// it to roughly once per minute — this will be badly wrong.
useEffect(() => {
  const id = setInterval(() => setSeconds((s) => s + 1), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(id);
}, []);
```

---

## The right way — compute from timestamps

```jsx
function Countdown({ targetTime, onComplete }) {
  const [remaining, setRemaining] = useState(() =>
    Math.max(0, targetTime - Date.now())
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    if (remaining <= 0) return;

    const id = setInterval(() => {
      const left = Math.max(0, targetTime - Date.now());   // ← recomputed, never accumulated
      setRemaining(left);
      if (left === 0) {
        clearInterval(id);
        onComplete?.();
      }
    }, 250);   // tick faster than the display so the second boundary looks crisp

    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [targetTime, onComplete, remaining > 0]);

  return <time>{format(remaining)}</time>;
}

function format(ms) {
  const total = Math.ceil(ms / 1000);
  const m = String(Math.floor(total / 60)).padStart(2, '0');
  const s = String(total % 60).padStart(2, '0');
  return `${m}:${s}`;
}
```

Because the value is derived from `Date.now()`, the timer is correct even after the tab has been backgrounded for ten minutes.

