## The one-line takeaway

> Timers are a **derived value from a timestamp**, not an accumulator. Every drift bug comes from treating them as an accumulator.

## Recursive rendering

```jsx
function Comment({ comment, depth = 0 }) {
  const [collapsed, setCollapsed] = useState(false);

  return (
    <li style={{ marginLeft: depth * 24 }}>
      <article>
        <strong>{comment.author}</strong>
        <p>{comment.body}</p>

        {comment.children.length > 0 && (
          <button
            onClick={() => setCollapsed((c) => !c)}
            aria-expanded={!collapsed}
          >
            {collapsed ? `Show ${comment.children.length} replies` : 'Hide replies'}
          </button>
        )}
      </article>

      {!collapsed && comment.children.length > 0 && (
        <ul>
          {comment.children.map((child) => (
            <Comment key={child.id} comment={child} depth={depth + 1} />
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </li>
  );
}
```

The component rendering itself is the whole mechanism. React handles arbitrary depth.

---

## Flat data → tree, in one pass

APIs usually return this:

```js
[
  { id: 1, parentId: null, body: 'Top level' },
  { id: 2, parentId: 1,    body: 'A reply' },
  { id: 3, parentId: 2,    body: 'Nested reply' },
]
```

```js
function buildTree(flat) {
  const map = new Map();
  const roots = [];

  // Pass 1: index every node and give it a children array
  for (const item of flat) {
    map.set(item.id, { ...item, children: [] });
  }

  // Pass 2: link each node to its parent
  for (const item of flat) {
    const node = map.get(item.id);
    if (item.parentId === null) {
      roots.push(node);
    } else {
      map.get(item.parentId)?.children.push(node);   // ?. handles orphans
    }
  }

  return roots;
}
```

**O(n).** The naive version — `flat.filter(c => c.parentId === id)` inside the recursion — is O(n²) and falls over on a few thousand comments. Say the complexity out loud.

Memoise it so it does not run on every render:

```jsx
const tree = useMemo(() => buildTree(comments), [comments]);
```

---

## Follow-ups

**"Very deep nesting breaks the layout."** Cap the visual indent (`Math.min(depth, 6) * 24`) and switch to a "continue this thread" link past a certain depth — what Reddit does.

**"Stack overflow risk?"** React's reconciler is iterative since Fiber, so rendering will not blow the stack. Your `buildTree` is iterative too. A recursive *traversal* function could overflow at extreme depth — convert to an explicit stack if that is a real concern.

**"10,000 comments?"** Flatten the visible tree into a list with depth values and virtualise it. Virtualising a genuine tree is hard; virtualising a flattened projection is straightforward.

**"Collapse state at scale?"** A `Set` of collapsed ids held at the top, not `useState` in each node — otherwise collapse state resets whenever a node unmounts.

**"Adding a reply?"** Update immutably along the path to the parent, or keep the flat array as the source of truth and rebuild the tree. The flat array is usually simpler.

