## What is being tested

Recursion, the O(n) versus O(n²) instinct, and whether you know where recursive UI stops scaling.

## Event delegation

```js
// ❌ 1,000 listeners, and new rows get none
document.querySelectorAll('.row').forEach((el) =>
  el.addEventListener('click', handle)
);

// ✅ One listener; works for rows added later
list.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const row = e.target.closest('[data-row-id]');
  if (!row || !list.contains(row)) return;
  handle(row.dataset.rowId);
});
```

`closest()` walks up from the actual target, so clicking a button *inside* the row still resolves to the row. The `list.contains` check guards against portaled content.

**This is exactly what React does** — one listener per event type on the root container, dispatched through the component tree. Saying that connects the concept to the framework.

---

## Pub/sub emitter

```js
class EventEmitter {
  #listeners = new Map();      // eventName → Set<callback>

  on(event, callback) {
    if (!this.#listeners.has(event)) this.#listeners.set(event, new Set());
    this.#listeners.get(event).add(callback);

    return () => this.off(event, callback);    // ← unsubscribe function
  }

  once(event, callback) {
    const unsubscribe = this.on(event, (...args) => {
      unsubscribe();
      callback(...args);
    });
    return unsubscribe;
  }

  off(event, callback) {
    const set = this.#listeners.get(event);
    if (!set) return;
    set.delete(callback);
    if (set.size === 0) this.#listeners.delete(event);
  }

  emit(event, ...args) {
    const set = this.#listeners.get(event);
    if (!set) return false;

    // Copy first — a listener may unsubscribe during iteration
    for (const cb of [...set]) {
      try {
        cb(...args);
      } catch (err) {
        console.error(`Error in "${event}" listener:`, err);   // one bad listener must not stop the rest
      }
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

---

## The three details that earn marks

**1. `on` returns an unsubscribe function.** Far less error-prone than requiring the caller to keep the exact reference for `off`.

**2. Copy the set before iterating.** A listener that calls `off` during `emit` would otherwise mutate the collection mid-iteration.

**3. `try/catch` per listener.** Without it, one throwing subscriber silently prevents every later one from running.

---

## Using it in React

```jsx
function useEvent(emitter, event, handler) {
  useEffect(() => {
    // `on` already returns the cleanup — pass it straight through
    return emitter.on(event, handler);
  }, [emitter, event, handler]);
}
```

That the unsubscribe function *is* the effect cleanup is a nice piece of API symmetry worth pointing out.

---

## `Set` versus array

`Set` gives O(1) removal and automatically ignores a duplicate registration of the same function. An array needs `indexOf` plus `splice` and will happily register the same callback twice.

## The flash problem

React renders after the bundle loads. If the theme is applied in an effect, the user sees a white flash first — worse on a slow connection, and it looks broken.

---

## The fix — a blocking script

```html
<!-- In <head>, BEFORE any stylesheet or bundle. Runs synchronously. -->
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      var stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      var system = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
      var theme = stored || (system ? 'dark' : 'light');
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = theme;
    } catch (e) {}
  })();
</script>
```

This is the rare case where a render-blocking script is correct — it is a few bytes and it runs before first paint.

In Next.js it goes in the root layout with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

---

## The React side

```jsx
const ThemeContext = createContext(null);

export function ThemeProvider({ children }) {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');   // matches SSR output

  // Read the value the blocking script already applied
  useEffect(() => {
    setTheme(document.documentElement.dataset.theme ?? 'light');
  }, []);

  const apply = useCallback((next) => {
    setTheme(next);
    document.documentElement.dataset.theme = next;
    localStorage.setItem('theme', next);
  }, []);

  // Follow the OS only while the user has not chosen explicitly
  useEffect(() => {
    const mq = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
    const onChange = (e) => {
      if (localStorage.getItem('theme')) return;
      const next = e.matches ? 'dark' : 'light';
      setTheme(next);
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = next;
    };
    mq.addEventListener('change', onChange);
    return () => mq.removeEventListener('change', onChange);
  }, []);

  const value = useMemo(() => ({ theme, setTheme: apply }), [theme, apply]);
  return <ThemeContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>;
}
```

Initialising state to `'light'` rather than reading the DOM keeps the server and client first render identical — no hydration mismatch. The effect corrects it immediately after.

