## The local-state exception for the text input

A controlled input driven straight from the URL feels laggy. Keep local state for the keystrokes and push to the URL debounced:

```jsx
function SearchInput({ value, onChange }) {
  const [local, setLocal] = useState(value);
  const debounced = useDebounced(local, 300);

  useEffect(() => { onChange(debounced); }, [debounced]);   // URL follows
  useEffect(() => { setLocal(value); }, [value]);           // back button follows

  return <input value={local} onChange={(e) => setLocal(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

Both effects are needed: one syncs typing into the URL, the other syncs back-navigation into the input.

---

## replace vs push

| Action | History | Why |
|--------|---------|-----|
| Typing in the search box | `replace` | Otherwise "laptop" leaves 6 history entries |
| Changing a filter | `push` | A deliberate action; back should undo it |
| Changing page | `push` | Back should return to the previous page |

Getting this wrong is the most visible bug in this exercise — press back once after typing and nothing appears to happen.

---

## Why bother

| Benefit | |
|---------|--|
| Shareable | Paste the link, colleague sees the same results |
| Bookmarkable | Saved searches for free |
| Back button works | Users expect it |
| Refresh-safe | State survives reload |
| Analytics | Popular filters visible in page-view data |
| SSR-friendly | The server can render the correct results directly |

